  "candidate_0030": {
    "reason": "The assertion guarantees a specific architecture (external document store) which is not strictly necessary for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). RAG can utilize internal vector stores, knowledge graphs, or other non-document-store sources. The literal text 'searches an external document store' is too specific; the correct standard is that it searches a 'retrievable corpus', which may be local,",
    "counterexample": "A system using an internal SQLite database as its retrieval source performs RAG without an 'external document store', refuting the necessity of the 'external' qualifier.",
    "sound_as_stated": false
  },
  "candidate_0031": {
    "reason": "The assertion implies a deterministic selection process ('selects') that guarantees a specific context set. However, retrieval is probabilistic and often involves ranking thresholds or top-k limits rather than a guaranteed 'selection' of all relevant items. The mechanism might fail to select any documents if the store is empty or relevance scores are below threshold.",
    "counterexample": "A query returns zero retrieved documents due to a high similarity threshold or an empty index, meaning no context set was formed despite the retriever component existing.",
    "sound_as_stated": false
  },
  "candidate_0032": {
    "reason": "The assertion assumes that injected context is always processed alongside the query in the transformer input. While common, some architectures use separate processing paths (e.g., parallel branches) or inject context via memory modules rather than direct concatenation into the standard transformer input sequence.",
    "counterexample": "A system using a hybrid architecture where retrieved documents are stored in an external memory bank accessed by attention mechanisms without being concatenated directly into the primary transformer input sequence.",
    "sound_as_stated": false
  },
  "candidate_0033": {
    "reason": "The assertion guarantees that self-attention is used and that synthesis occurs. While transformers typically use self-attention, other architectures (e.g., RNNs with external memory) can also perform RAG-like tasks. Furthermore, 'synthesizing information' is a high-level description; the actual mechanism might be simple concatenation without deep semantic synthesis if the model lacks capacity.",
    "counterexample": "A system using a non-transformer architecture (e.g., a large language model based on sparse attention or a different neural network topology) that still performs retrieval-augmented generation, refuting the necessity of 'self-attention' and standard transformer inputs.",
    "sound_as_stated": false
  }
}
